+## Branch Divergence Analysis
+
+### Cause
+While the autonomous agent consolidation was being performed locally, the remote branch received 17 commits with different work. This created a divergence where neither branch can be fast-forwarded to the other.
+
+### Impact
+- Cannot push local commits without resolving divergence
+- Cannot pull remote commits without handling conflicts
+- Must choose a resolution strategy
+
+---
+
+## Resolution Options
+
+### Option 1: Force Push (Recommended - Clean History)
+
+**Action**: Force push the consolidated commit to remote, discarding remote work
+```bash
+git push --force-with-lease origin feature/sqlite-3.52-modernization
+```
+
+**Pros**:
+- Clean, linear history with single consolidated commit
+- Preserves the carefully crafted consolidation
+- All work is included in the consolidated commit
+- Easier to understand project history
+
+**Cons**:
+- Discards the 17 remote commits (but their work is in the consolidated commit)
+- Rewrites remote history (requires coordination if others are using it)
+- Cannot be undone without backup
+
+**When to use**: If the remote commits' work is already included in the consolidated commit, or if clean history is more important than preserving remote commit granularity.
+
+### Option 2: Pull and Rebase (Interactive)
+
+**Action**: Rebase local commits on top of remote
+```bash
+git pull --rebase origin feature/sqlite-3.52-modernization
+# Resolve conflicts interactively
+```
+
+**Pros**:
+- Preserves both local and remote work
+- Creates linear history
+- Git helps identify conflicts
+
+**Cons**:
+- May have significant merge conflicts
+- Complicated with large consolidated commit
+- Could break the consolidated commit structure
+
+**When to use**: If remote has unique work not in the consolidated commit and you want to keep granular history.
+
+### Option 3: Pull and Merge (Preserve Both)
+
+**Action**: Merge remote into local
+```bash
+git pull origin feature/sqlite-3.52-modernization
+# Create merge commit
+```
+
+**Pros**:
+- Preserves all history
+- No history rewriting
+- Safe operation
+
+**Cons**:
+- Creates merge commit
+- More complex history
+- May have conflicts to resolve
+
+**When to use**: If you want to preserve both branches' histories completely.
+
+### Option 4: Create New Branch (Conservative)
+
+**Action**: Push consolidated commit to a new branch
+```bash
+git checkout -b feature/sqlite-3.52-consolidated
+git push origin feature/sqlite-3.52-consolidated
+```
+
+**Pros**:
+- Preserves remote branch unchanged
+- Allows comparison of both approaches
+- No destructive operations
+
+**Cons**:
+- Creates branch proliferation
+- Delays resolution decision
+- Requires eventual merge/decision
+
+**When to use**: If uncertain about best approach or need to review both branches.
+
